      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)?$",
      "worked_solution": "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $11\\cdot (-3)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35+11\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35-33\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
